## Why Build a Ray Tracer?

Ray tracing is one of those topics where the gap between theory and implementation feels satisfying to close. I had read the classic *Ray Tracing in One Weekend* series and wanted to push further — writing something in Rust that could eventually grow into a full PBRT implementation.

The result is [ray-tracing](https://github.com/ratnajagadeesharava/ray-tracing), my Rust-based renderer. This post covers the core ideas and design choices.

## The Core Idea

A ray tracer simulates light by casting rays from a camera through each pixel and computing what they hit. For every intersection, you evaluate a *shading model* — how much light arrives at that point and in what direction it scatters.

```rust
pub struct Ray {
    pub origin: Vec3,
    pub direction: Vec3,
}

impl Ray {
    pub fn at(&self, t: f64) -> Vec3 {
        self.origin + t * self.direction
    }
}
```

## Bounding Volume Hierarchy

Naively intersecting every ray against every object is O(n) per pixel. For a scene with thousands of triangles, this kills performance. A BVH tree reduces this to O(log n) by recursively partitioning the scene into axis-aligned bounding boxes.

```rust
enum BVHNode {
    Leaf(Box<dyn Hittable>),
    Branch {
        left: Box<BVHNode>,
        right: Box<BVHNode>,
        bbox: AABB,
    },
}
```

Building the BVH involves sorting objects by their centroid along the axis with the largest spread — a strategy that keeps the tree balanced for typical scenes.

## Materials

Rust's trait system is perfect for polymorphic materials. Each material implements a `scatter` method:

```rust
pub trait Material: Send + Sync {
    fn scatter(
        &self,
        ray: &Ray,
        hit: &HitRecord,
    ) -> Option<(Ray, Color)>;
}
```

I implemented Lambertian diffuse, metal with controllable fuzz, and dielectrics (glass) with Schlick approximation for Fresnel reflectance.

## Multi-threading with Rayon

One of Rust's killer features is fearless concurrency. Adding parallelism was literally four lines:

```rust
use rayon::prelude::*;

image.par_chunks_mut(width * 3)
     .enumerate()
     .for_each(|(j, row)| {
         render_row(j, row, &world, &camera);
     });
```

This gave an 8× speedup on my 8-core machine with zero data races.

## What's Next

The renderer currently handles spheres and diffuse/specular/glass materials. Future work:
- Triangle meshes with Möller–Trumbore intersection
- Area lights and next event estimation
- Path tracing with Russian roulette termination
- Physically-based BRDFs (GGX microfacet model)

Building this gave me a far deeper appreciation for how GPUs and real-time renderers like Unreal approach these same problems under tight time budgets.

## The Promise of Reactive

When I joined Brane Enterprises, the Angular codebase was held together by a mix of `subscribe()` calls, manual state flags, and unpredictable loading spinners. Three years later, a consistent RxJS discipline changed everything. This post distills what worked.

## Anti-pattern: Nested Subscribes

The most common mistake beginners make:

```typescript
// ❌ Don't do this
this.userService.getUser(id).subscribe(user => {
  this.postService.getPosts(user.id).subscribe(posts => {
    this.posts = posts; // callback hell
  });
});
```

Use `switchMap` instead:

```typescript
// ✅ Flat, composable
this.userId$.pipe(
  switchMap(id => this.userService.getUser(id)),
  switchMap(user => this.postService.getPosts(user.id))
).subscribe(posts => this.posts = posts);
```

## Pattern: Smart Observable State

For complex UI state, build observables that derive from a single source of truth:

```typescript
class DashboardComponent {
  private refresh$ = new BehaviorSubject<void>(undefined);

  data$ = this.refresh$.pipe(
    switchMap(() => this.api.fetchDashboard()),
    shareReplay(1)
  );

  loading$ = merge(
    this.refresh$.pipe(map(() => true)),
    this.data$.pipe(map(() => false))
  );

  error$ = this.data$.pipe(
    catchError(err => of(err)),
    filter(v => v instanceof Error)
  );

  refresh() { this.refresh$.next(); }
}
```

The template becomes purely declarative:

```html
<ng-container *ngIf="data$ | async as data; else loading">
  <dashboard-view [data]="data" />
</ng-container>
```

## Pattern: Unified CRUD with EntityStore

For our analytical dashboards, we built a generic entity store pattern:

```typescript
class EntityStore<T extends { id: string }> {
  private _entities$ = new BehaviorSubject<Map<string, T>>(new Map());

  entities$ = this._entities$.pipe(
    map(m => Array.from(m.values()))
  );

  upsert(entity: T) {
    const current = this._entities$.value;
    this._entities$.next(new Map(current).set(entity.id, entity));
  }

  remove(id: string) {
    const current = new Map(this._entities$.value);
    current.delete(id);
    this._entities$.next(current);
  }
}
```

## Managing Subscriptions with takeUntilDestroyed

Angular 16+ ships `takeUntilDestroyed()` which replaced our custom `destroy$` subject pattern:

```typescript
// Angular 16+
this.data$.pipe(
  takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef)
).subscribe(...);
```

## Key Takeaways

1. **Never nest subscribes** — use `switchMap`, `mergeMap`, `concatMap`
2. **Derive state, don't store it** — compute loading/error from the same stream
3. **Use `shareReplay(1)`** for data shared across templates
4. **Always unsubscribe** — use `async` pipe or `takeUntilDestroyed`
5. **Prefer `combineLatest`** over multiple separate subscriptions for dependent data

These patterns cut our component code by ~40% and eliminated a whole class of race condition bugs.

## The Brief

At Brane Enterprises, we needed to evaluate whether a custom engine could replace Unity for our industrial visualization pipeline. I had three months to prototype and decide. This is what I learned.

## Why Build Your Own Engine (Briefly)

Custom engines aren't ego projects for teams with a legitimate use case. Our reasons:
- **Deterministic asset pipeline** — Unity's asset import is a black box for our custom formats
- **Rendering control** — we needed deferred rendering with custom G-buffer passes
- **No runtime license fees** at scale

## Architecture: Entity-Component-System

I evaluated three patterns — OOP scene graphs, ECS, and DOD component arrays. For our use case, a simple ECS with sparse component storage won:

```cpp
class World {
public:
    EntityId create();
    void destroy(EntityId id);

    template<typename T>
    T& add(EntityId id);

    template<typename T>
    T* get(EntityId id);

    template<typename... Components, typename Fn>
    void each(Fn&& fn);
};
```

The key insight: most systems only care about 2-3 components. `each<Transform, Mesh, Material>()` iterates only entities that have all three, with cache-friendly memory layout.

## OpenGL Rendering Pipeline

I implemented a deferred renderer with three passes:
1. **G-buffer pass** — store position, normal, albedo, metallic/roughness to textures
2. **Lighting pass** — fullscreen quad reads G-buffer, evaluates PBR BRDF per light
3. **Post-processing** — bloom, tone mapping, FXAA

```glsl
// Fragment shader for G-buffer
layout(location = 0) out vec3 gPosition;
layout(location = 1) out vec3 gNormal;
layout(location = 2) out vec4 gAlbedoSpec;

void main() {
    gPosition = fragPos;
    gNormal = normalize(normal);
    gAlbedoSpec.rgb = texture(albedoMap, TexCoords).rgb;
    gAlbedoSpec.a = texture(specularMap, TexCoords).r;
}
```

## The Rabbit Holes

**Hot-reloading shaders** — I spent a week building shader hot-reload. Worth it for iteration speed, but the file-watching code was surprisingly gnarly on Windows (ReadDirectoryChangesW is a lesson in Win32 API design philosophy).

**Asset serialization** — I chose a custom binary format for speed, then spent three days debugging byte-alignment issues. Lesson: use a proven format (like GLTF 2.0) until you have a real reason not to.

**Editor tooling** — I underestimated how much time an engine *editor* takes. ImGui saved me here. It's not pretty but it shipped fast.

## The Verdict

After three months: a working prototype with a deferred renderer, ECS world, basic physics (via Bullet), and a rudimentary editor. Fast enough for our use case, but the maintenance cost was underestimated.

**What I'd do differently:**
- Adopt GLTF from day one
- Start with a fixed-function renderer, add flexibility later
- Invest in the editor earlier — you can't tune what you can't see
- Consider Godot before rolling your own

The exercise was deeply educational. Even if the engine never ships, the knowledge of what Unity and Unreal are doing under the hood is invaluable.
